.TH "NETCHECK" "1" "2026\-11\-18" "netcheck" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
netcheck \- check network connectivity from this host
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]netcheck\f[R] [\f[I]OPTIONS\f[R]] [\f[I]BUNDLE\f[R]\&...]
.PP
\f[B]netcheck version\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]|\f[B]\-\-verbose\f[R]]
[\f[B]\-D\f[R]|\f[B]\-\-dotenv\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]]
.PP
\f[B]netcheck completion\f[R] \f[I]SHELL\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]netcheck \-\-version\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]netcheck\f[R] tests whether this host can reach other endpoints.
A \f[I]bundle\f[R] is a list of checks, written in JSON or YAML, with
timeouts, retries and a concurrency.
\f[B]netcheck\f[R] runs the bundles in the order given, one after the
other; the checks of one bundle run concurrently.
It tests TCP, UDP, ICMP, TLS, DTLS, SSH, HTTP, HTTPS, PostgreSQL (its
TLS negotiation) and SPIFFE servers (a TLS server verified by its SVID),
and a TLS check also verifies the server certificate and its expiry.
.PP
Every argument that is not an option is the location of a bundle.
With no bundle, \f[B]netcheck\f[R] needs a template and runs it on mock
data, to help write the template.
.PP
Options that take a value are written with an equals sign
(\f[B]\-\-format=json\f[R]) or glued to the short option
(\f[B]\-fjson\f[R]); \f[B]\-f json\f[R] does not take \f[I]json\f[R] as
its value.
.PP
Ctrl\-C ends the run: the checks that had not finished are reported as
skipped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-f\f[R], \f[B]\-\-format\f[R]=\f[I]FORMAT\f[R]
How to write the report: \f[B]json\f[R], \f[B]yaml\f[R], \f[B]text\f[R]
(the default) or \f[B]template\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-t\f[R], \f[B]\-\-template\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
A Go template for the report; it implies \f[B]\-\-format=template\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-print\-diagnostics\f[R]
Print the report\(cqs diagnostics to the standard error.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-allow\-net\f[R]=\f[I]NET\f[R]
Only connect to this network (CIDR or address); repeatable.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-deny\-net\f[R]=\f[I]NET\f[R]
Never connect to this network (CIDR or address); repeatable, and it wins
over \f[B]\-\-allow\-net\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-\-spiffe\-bundle\f[R]=\f[I]FILE\f[R]
The trust bundle, a PEM file of the CAs of a SPIFFE trust domain, that
verifies the servers of \f[B]spire\f[R] checks; read at each attempt.
Environment: \f[B]NETCHECK_SPIFFE_BUNDLE\f[R].
Without it a \f[B]spire\f[R] check fails.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-fetch\-timeout\f[R]=\f[I]DURATION\f[R]
The longest time to load one bundle; \f[B]0\f[R] means no limit.
The default is \f[B]30s\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Show the help message.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print the version and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\f[B]version\f[R] [\f[B]\-v\f[R]] [\f[B]\-D\f[R] \f[I]FILE\f[R]]
Print one line with the name and the version; with \f[B]\-v\f[R] the
build details too.
\f[B]\-D\f[R] (\f[B]\-\-dotenv\f[R]) loads a \f[I].env\f[R] file and
lists the \f[B]NETCHECK_*\f[R] variables, with the values of passwords,
tokens and secrets masked.
.TP
\f[B]completion\f[R] \f[I]SHELL\f[R]
Print the completion script for \f[B]bash\f[R], \f[B]zsh\f[R],
\f[B]fish\f[R] or \f[B]powershell\f[R].
The scripts are static: completing never runs \f[B]netcheck\f[R].
.SH BUNDLE SOURCES
The scheme of a location picks its source; a location with none of these
schemes is a file name.
.TP
\f[I]file\f[R]
A file, in JSON or YAML.
.TP
\f[B]http://\f[R], \f[B]https://\f[R]
HTTP and HTTPS, the certificate verified.
\f[B]https\-://\f[R] does not verify the certificate; use it only for
tests.
.TP
\f[B]http+sso://\f[R], \f[B]https+sso://\f[R]
The same, authenticating with the local Kerberos or NTLM identity.
.TP
\f[B]redis://\f[R], \f[B]rediss://\f[R]
A Redis key: \f[I]redis://[user:pass\(at]host[:port]?key=K[&db=N]\f[R];
\f[B]rediss://\f[R] is over TLS.
.TP
\f[B]consulkv://\f[R], \f[B]consulkvs://\f[R]
A Consul KV key:
\f[I]consulkv://[user:pass\(at]host[:port]?key=K[&dc=D]\f[R];
\f[B]consulkvs://\f[R] is over TLS.
.TP
\f[B]consulsr://\f[R], \f[B]consulsrs://\f[R]
A Consul service\(cqs metadata:
\f[I]consulsr://[user:pass\(at]host[:port]?service=S&tag=T&meta=M[&dc=D]\f[R].
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Every check of every bundle passed; also \f[B]\-\-help\f[R] and
\f[B]version\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
The command line is invalid, a template or a bundle could not be loaded
or is invalid, the output could not be written, or the run was
interrupted.
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
At least one check failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]./netcheck.conf\f[R], \f[I]\(ti/netcheck.conf\f[R], \f[I]/etc/netcheck.conf\f[R]
The defaults file, YAML: timeout, retries, wait, concurrency and the
ping settings.
The first one found is read; the settings a bundle or a check gives win.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
\f[B]NETCHECK_LOG_LEVEL\f[R]
\f[B]debug\f[R], \f[B]info\f[R], \f[B]warn\f[R], \f[B]error\f[R] or
\f[B]off\f[R]; unset means off.
.TP
\f[B]NETCHECK_LOG_STREAM\f[R]
\f[B]stderr\f[R] (the default), \f[B]stdout\f[R] or \f[B]file\f[R],
which writes \f[I]netcheck\-PID.log\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]NETCHECK_CPU_PROFILE\f[R], \f[B]NETCHECK_MEM_PROFILE\f[R]
Write a CPU or memory profile to the file named.
.TP
\f[B]NETCHECK_SPIFFE_BUNDLE\f[R]
The trust bundle of \f[B]\-\-spiffe\-bundle\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]NETCHECK_DOTENV\f[R]
Load that \f[I].env\f[R] file first; it may set the variables above.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run a bundle and read the report as JSON:
.IP
.EX
netcheck \-\-format=json bundle.yaml
.EE
.PP
Run a bundle kept on a server, refusing to connect outside 10.0.0.0/8:
.IP
.EX
netcheck \-\-allow\-net=10.0.0.0/8 https://config.example.org/bundle.yaml
.EE
.PP
Use the status in a script:
.IP
.EX
netcheck bundle.yaml || alert
.EE
.PP
Load the completion for bash in the current shell:
.IP
.EX
source <(netcheck completion bash)
.EE
